WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
As with any comic book movie, "The Suicide Squad"&nbsp;
features some comic book deep cuts. A few of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.720
are villains with larger backstories, a&nbsp;
couple have more nuance than is shown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.200
and one is a big-time important cameo. Here's&nbsp;
how these characters mean more than you realize.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.160
Calendar Man, played by Sean Gunn, shows&nbsp;
up as a prisoner who mocks Polka-Dot Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.920
Given the events of "The Suicide Squad," a little&nbsp;
comics know-how shows that this foreshadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.440
Polka-Dot Man's evolution over the movie.
Calendar Man's calling card is that he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.920
obsessed with calendars and dates, often planning&nbsp;
his crimes on days of specific significance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.120
There's no way or need to sugar coat this: that&nbsp;
is an extremely stupid gimmick for a villain. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.920
among Batman's rogues' gallery, which includes a&nbsp;
man who loves riddles, he may represent the nadir.&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.080
Or at least, he represented that nadir until the&nbsp;
Batman story "The Long Halloween" which finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.920
Batman tracking down a mysterious killer&nbsp;
who's murdering a new victim every month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.200
and the only person who knows the&nbsp;
killer's identity is Calendar Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.880
In a role often compared to that of Hannibal&nbsp;
Lecter in "Silence of the Lambs," Calendar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.880
Man drops hints from his cell in Arkham Asylum.
Much like the way "The Long Halloween" proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.880
Calendar Man's worth, "The Suicide Squad" is&nbsp;
the vehicle that elevates Polka-Dot Man beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.360
joke status. Gunn included Polka-Dot Man after&nbsp;
Googling "dumbest supervillain of all time" -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.840
proceeded to give the formerly maligned villain&nbsp;
a compelling backstory. Calendar Man is laughing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.434
at what he once was.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:16.960
"I'm a superhero!"

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.040
Double Down, played by Jared Leland Gore, joins&nbsp;
Calendar Man in his mockery of Polka-Dot Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.200
Unlike Calendar Man, however, Double Down has&nbsp;
never had a reinvention or reintroduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.280
and has remained a throwaway gimmick villain.&nbsp;
Whether Gunn wanted another goofy villain to laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.840
at Polka-Dot Man or is setting up a potential&nbsp;
future Task Force X member is hard to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.840
but his goofiness is worth acknowledging.
Double Down, in his limited appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.560
has largely been a villain who battles the Flash.&nbsp;
He was a con man and semi-professional gambler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.720
because nothing makes for a good villain origin&nbsp;
like three card monte. He encountered a cursed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.960
deck of cards, which gave him his powers -- all&nbsp;
of which are weird card gimmicks, most of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.960
involve throwing them. Listen, we promised&nbsp;
interesting, we didn't promise fleshed out.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:56.880
Doctor Fitzgibbon is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.280
of the doctors working for ARGUS. Among his many&nbsp;
duties are inserting the nanite bombs that act as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.000
Waller's insurance into the skulls of Task Force&nbsp;
X members. The character is only glimpsed briefly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.600
but the role itself isn't important.&nbsp;
Rather, it's the person playing him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.400
John Ostrander, the man responsible for some of&nbsp;
the best and most important Suicide Squad comics.&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.880
Gunn noted on Twitter that&nbsp;
Ostrander is a former actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.840
which made the role easier. Gunn&nbsp;
is also on record saying that&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.600
"Ostrander's Suicide Squad is the biggest&nbsp;
influence on the film (along with Aldrich's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.360
"The Dirty Dozen") ... We had a huge poster of&nbsp;
this in our production office while shooting."

00:02:28.475 --> 00:02:33.280
John Economos, played by comedian Steve Agee, is&nbsp;
the warden of Belle Reve. It's a minor role -- he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.960
shows up long enough to prove he's competent&nbsp;
at what he does and that he hates Peacemaker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.000
He has a similar role on the comics, debuting&nbsp;
in "Suicide Squad" #1 (1987) with the same job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.520
and has shown up two or three times each&nbsp;
decade since the end of Ostrander's run.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.280
There's admittedly not a ton of depth to his&nbsp;
character, but it's not his character that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.720
important. Rather, it's Agee himself,&nbsp;
who pulls double duty in this movie:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.480
he was also the stand-in for King&nbsp;
Shark. Agee told ComicBook.com,&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.080
"I was tall. [Gunn] needed somebody tall.&nbsp;
Like, literally needed somebody tall."

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.120
When Task Force X stops by a bar to grab a few&nbsp;
drinks, they find a few dozen other people there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.440
with them. One, however, stands out above the&nbsp;
rest. There's a man with white hair seen dancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.120
with several women. Though the character is&nbsp;
never given a name, the actor playing him is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.520
one of the most important people in James Gunn's&nbsp;
career: schlock horror legend Lloyd Kaufman.&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.080
Kaufman is the founder of Troma Entertainment,&nbsp;
best known for bringing us the Toxic Avenger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.760
Gunn's film career started at Troma in the&nbsp;
'90s, when he co-wrote the screenplay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.160
"Tromeo and Juliet." Gunn once tweeted that
"Troma was a crash course in everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.480
about making movies, from writing a script to&nbsp;
casting to location scouting to production to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.760
marketing the film. Without Lloyd Kaufman,&nbsp;
I certainly wouldn’t have the all-around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.920
education I needed to be a film director."
Gunn has repaid Kaufman by giving his mentor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.960
cameos, including a small part&nbsp;
in "Guardians of the Galaxy."&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.440
Kaufman once told Vice that
"[Gunn] put me in all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.280
movies so far and now he's the number one&nbsp;
guy at the box office! Like that Titanic guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.440
you know? 'I'm the king of the world!' Only James&nbsp;
would never say that cause he's such a nice guy."

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.560
The first Ratcatcher only appears in two scenes,&nbsp;
albeit two of the most emotional scenes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.200
"The Suicide Squad." Played by Taika Waititi,&nbsp;
Ratcatcher is often spoken of in reverent tones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.520
throughout the film -- or at least as reverent&nbsp;
as people can be about an ostensible villain who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.400
controls rats. Like many of the characters in "The&nbsp;
Suicide Squad," Ratcatcher is a reimagining of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.040
low-tier DC Comics villain of the same name.
The Ratcatcher of the comics comes from Gotham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.520
City rather than Portugal. He was an actual&nbsp;
rat catcher by trade before, like seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.320
half of Gotham City, he turned to crime. He also&nbsp;
had the ability to speak with and control rats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.400
but his most valuable ability was his&nbsp;
encyclopedic knowledge of Gotham's sewer system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.160
which let him move around the city with&nbsp;
ease. He's a much less vulnerable figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.920
than we see in "The Suicide Squad."
 
